https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/openssl-fixes-high-severity-dtls-flaw.html	OpenSSL Fixes High-Severity DTLS Flaw That Can Leak Heap Memory Unencrypted	https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/openssl-fixes-high-severity-dtls-flaw.html	The Hacker News	2026-09-30 08:09:28+00	2026-10-02 16:05:30.97+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)", "snippet": "A High-severity OpenSSL flaw can leak heap memory to the other side of a DTLS connection or crash the program, OpenSSL said on September 29 as it released fixes.\\n\\nDTLS, the TLS variant used for UDP traffic, resends a handshake message if no reply arrives before the timer expires. The leak or crash can happen when such a resend starts while a larger handshake message is stuck part-way"}	verified	[]	OpenSSL has released fixes for a High-severity flaw affecting the DTLS variant. The flaw allows leaked heap memory or program crashes to occur if a resend of a handshake message happens while the original message is still partially written.	["High-severity OpenSSL flaw", "Affects DTLS variant for UDP traffic", "Leak or crash can occur if resend happens during part of a handshake message", "Fixes released by OpenSSL on September 29"]	["OpenSSL", "DTLS", "TCP", "High-severity flaw", "Heap memory leak", "Program crash"]	["OpenSSL", "DTLS", "TCP"]	["Computer Security", "Networking", "Security Flaw", "High-severity"]	high	[]
