https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/kiteworks-urges-customers-to-shut-down.html	Kiteworks Urges Customers to Shut Down Systems for 9 Hours Over Possible Cyber Attack	https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/kiteworks-urges-customers-to-shut-down.html	The Hacker News	2026-09-26 07:48:33+00	2026-09-30 16:05:31.357+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)", "snippet": "Kiteworks (formerly Accellion) is urging customers to shut down their systems as a precautionary measure for nine hours over the weekend after it received threat intelligence about an imminent cyber attack.\\n\\n\\"Kiteworks received credible threat intelligence from federal intelligence authorities indicating that a threat actor may attempt to target some Kiteworks systems,\\" said Frank Balonis, Chief"}	verified	[]	Kiteworks, formerly known as Accellion, is asking its customers to momentarily shut down their systems for nine hours due to a potential cyber attack. The company was notified by federal intelligence agencies that a threat actor might attempt to target Kiteworks' systems.	["Kiteworks asks customers to shut down systems", "Precautionary measure for nine hours", "Imminent cyber attack suspected"]	["Kiteworks", "Accellion", "federal intelligence agencies"]	["cybersecurity", "system shut down"]	["cybersecurity incident", "cyber attack", "government notification"]	medium	[]
https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/compromised-github-actions-came-back.html	Compromised GitHub Actions Came Back Online and Resumed Executing Mini Shai-Hulud Malware	https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/compromised-github-actions-came-back.html	The Hacker News	2026-09-25 14:44:41+00	2026-09-30 16:05:31.357+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)", "snippet": "Two actions-cool GitHub Actions have been disabled for a second time after the repositories became accessible last week, months after they were compromised during the May 2026 Mini Shai-Hulud campaign.\\n\\nThe affected GitHub Actions are listed below -\\n\\n\\n  actions-cool/issues-helper\\n  actions-cool/maintain-one-comment\\n\\nVisiting either of the repositories now shows the message: \\"Access to this"}	verified	[]	Two GitHub Actions repositories, originally compromised in the Mini Shai-Hulud malware campaign, have been disabled again after becoming accessible. They continue to execute the malware, despite previous suspensions, following a second disablement attempt.	["GitHub Actions compromised again after becoming accessible", "Repositories previously compromised in 2026 Mini Shai-Hulud campaign", "Current access to repositories still allows malware execution"]	["actions-cool/maintain-one-comment", "actions-cool/issues-helper"]	["GitHub Actions", "Mini Shai-Hulud"]	["cybersecurity", "malware", "web_security"]	high	[]
https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/pamstealer-macos-malware-adds-live-c2.html	PamStealer macOS Malware Adds Live C2 Payload Decryption and Multi-Layer Persistence	https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/pamstealer-macos-malware-adds-live-c2.html	The Hacker News	2026-09-25 13:18:06+00	2026-09-30 16:05:31.357+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)", "snippet": "Cybersecurity researchers have flagged a new version of PamStealer that ensures that the main payload can only be recovered using a server-side decryption chain.\\n\\nThe latest artifacts, per Jamf Threat Labs, continue to rely on the same JavaScript for Automation (JXA) dropper mechanism, but modify the lure and the delivery method.\\n\\n\\"Where earlier variants embedded their payload key material"}	verified	[]	A new version of the PamStealer macOS malware has been detected. This version includes live C2 (Command and Control) payload decryption and multi-layer persistence. The malware leverages the same JXA dropper as previous versions but changes the methods for lure embedding and distribution. The malware still uses the original key material embedding technique.	["New version of PamStealer detected", "Includes live C2 payload decryption and multi-layer persistence", "Uses the same JavaScript for Automation (JXA) dropper mechanism as previous versions", "Changes lure embedding and delivery method", "Embeds the key material in the same manner as earlier variants"]	["PamStealer macOS Malware", "JavaScript for Automation (JXA) dropper", "Live C2 payload decryption", "Multi-layer persistence", "Lure embedding and delivery"]	["JavaScript for Automation (JXA)", "MacOS"]	["macOS malware", "JavaScript for Automation (JXA)", "C2 (Command and Control)", "Persistence mechanisms", "Leverage of unchanged dropper"]	high	[]
https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/bitget-says-suspected-north-korean.html	Bitget Says Suspected North Korean Hackers Stole $351.6M After Backend Compromise	https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/bitget-says-suspected-north-korean.html	The Hacker News	2026-09-25 10:35:55+00	2026-09-30 16:05:31.357+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)", "snippet": "Cryptocurrency exchange Bitget said suspected North Korean threat actors have stolen $351.6 million from its hot and warm wallets. \\n\\n\\"At 18:31 UTC on September 24, 2026, Bitget's security systems identified unauthorized transfers involving a limited number of hot wallets,\\" Bitget said in a post shared on X. \\"Bitget's cold wallets and the overwhelming majority of platform assets remain"}	verified	[]	Cryptocurrency exchange Bitget reported that suspected North Korean hackers stole $351.6 million from its hot and warm wallets after a backend compromise. The incident was detected on September 24, 2026, with unauthorized hot wallet activities being identified.	["351.6 million dollars stolen from hot and warm wallets", "Identified by a backend compromise and detection of unauthorized transfers", "Cold wallets and majority of platform assets remain unaffected"]	["Bitget", "351.6 million dollars", "North Korea", "September 24, 2026", "X (a platform sharing the news)"]	["Cryptocurrency exchange", "Blockchain technology", "Cybersecurity", "Web post (X platform)"]	["Cybersecurity breach", "Cryptocurrency theft", "North Korean threat actors", "Backend compromise"]	high	[]
https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/cloudflare-fixes-flaw-that-let-one.html	Cloudflare Fixes Flaw That Let One Container Read Another Customer's Leftover Disk Data	https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/cloudflare-fixes-flaw-that-let-one.html	The Hacker News	2026-09-25 04:49:22+00	2026-09-30 16:05:31.357+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)", "snippet": "A flaw in Cloudflare Containers let a paying customer read data that other customers' containers had left behind on the same server, Cloudflare and the researchers who found it said on Thursday.\\n\\nThe data came from disk space that earlier containers had used and given up, not from any live workload, and an attacker could not choose whose data they got, according to Cloudflare. The company"}	verified	[]	Cloudflare has addressed a security flaw in their Containers product that allowed one paying customer to read leftover disk data from another customer's containers stored on the same server. The data was not from active workloads but from previously used disk space, and an attacker could not select whose data they accessed.	["Security flaw in Cloudflare Containers", "Access to residual customer data from disk space used by other containers", "Data not from live workloads, but from unused disk space", "Attackers could not choose whose data to access"]	["Cloudflare", "Containers product", "Security flaw"]	["Cloudflare"]	["Security", "Data leakage", "Container security", "Disk space issues"]	medium	[]
https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/unpatched-oneplus-flaws-let-installed.html	Unpatched OnePlus Flaws Let Installed Android Apps Gain Root Without Permissions	https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/unpatched-oneplus-flaws-let-installed.html	The Hacker News	2026-09-24 18:10:18+00	2026-09-30 16:05:31.357+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)", "snippet": "A OnePlus 15 running the latest OxygenOS can be rooted by a malicious app the owner installs, one that asks for no special permissions. A researcher, Rasmus Moorats, chained two flaws in OnePlus's own software to gain root access, the highest level of control over an Android phone.\\n\\nOnePlus told him the same flaws affect many more of its own devices and those of OPPO, though it has not"}	verified	[]	A flaw in OnePlus's latest OxygenOS version allows a malicious app to gain root access to an Android device without the user's permission. A researcher named Rasmus Maurats discovered two vulnerabilities in OnePlus's software that can be exploited to execute root-level operations on affected devices.	["OnePlus device can be rooted by apps installed by the user", "The vulnerabilities were chained together by the researcher", "The issue affects many OnePlus and Oppo devices", "OnePlus has acknowledged the flaws but has not initiated a fix"]	["Rasmus Maurats", "OnePlus", "OxygenOS", "OPPO"]	["Android", "OxygenOS", "Linux"]	["Mobile Security", "Software Flaws", "Rooting", "Security Research"]	high	[]
https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/placeholder-third-partycom-referenced.html	Placeholder third-party[.]com Referenced Across 1,700+ Repositories Now Serves Malicious Content	https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/placeholder-third-partycom-referenced.html	The Hacker News	2026-09-24 15:27:32+00	2026-09-30 16:05:31.357+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)", "snippet": "The \\"third-party[.]com\\" domain, commonly used as a documentation placeholder, has been observed serving a ClickFix lure to Windows browsers while displaying a harmless decoy to other users.\\n\\n\\"third-party[.]com has been a generic documentation placeholder for years, the same role example.com plays,\\" Manifold Security's Head of Research, Ax Sharma, said. \\"Unlike 'example[.]com,' third-party[.]com"}	verified	[]	The 'third-party[.]com' domain, often used for placeholder documentation, has been found serving a malicious ClickFix lure to Windows browsers, despite displaying a harmless decoy to other users. This domain has maintained its placeholder function for years, similar to example.com.	["third-party[.]com domain was observed serving malicious ClickFix lure content", "Content displayed as a decoy to other users", "Domain has been a placeholder for years, similar to example.com"]	["third-party[.]com", "ClickFix", "Windows"]	["HTML", "JavaScript", "Windows browsers"]	["security", "malware", "cybersecurity"]	medium	[]
https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/corp-mdm-spyware-targets-logistics.html	Corp MDM Spyware Targets Logistics Firms, Steals New SMS and Redirects Calls	https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/corp-mdm-spyware-targets-logistics.html	The Hacker News	2026-09-24 12:05:27+00	2026-09-30 16:05:31.357+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)", "snippet": "The logistics sector has become the target of a new malicious cyber campaign that distributes an Android spyware codenamed Corp MDM.\\n\\nAccording to Have I Been Squatted, the campaign uses fake Google Play pages branded as CEVA and TKW Logistics to distribute an Android Package Kit (APK) file that's dressed up as a system service. The delivered app has the package name \\"com.corp.mdm\\"\\n\\nCorp MDM"}	verified	[]	A new malicious cyber campaign targeting the logistics sector has been discovered, deploying an Android spyware called Corp MDM. The campaign involves fake Google Play pages, distributing an APK file disguised as a system service. The fake app is labeled 'com.corp.mdm'.	["New malicious campaign targeting logistics firms", "Uses fake Google Play pages with branded names (CEVA, TKW Logistics)", "Distributes fake APK file as a system service", "Delivered app package name is 'com.corp.mdm'"]	["Corp MDM", "logistics sector", "Android spyware", "fake Google Play pages", "CEVA", "TKW Logistics", "fake APK file", "system service"]	["Android", "Google Play", "APK", "system service"]	["malware", "cyber attack", "supply chain"]	high	[]
https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/17000-urls-reveal-how-clickfix-turns.html	17,000 URLs Reveal How ClickFix Turns Trusted Websites Into Malware Traps: Report by CTM360	https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/17000-urls-reveal-how-clickfix-turns.html	The Hacker News	2026-09-24 09:14:21+00	2026-09-30 16:05:31.357+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)", "snippet": "ClickFix has become the most common way attackers get into enterprise networks, and it does it without an exploit, an attachment, or a file on disk. Our new global threat report traces the technique from a novelty in late 2023 to a subscription product with on-chain infrastructure and a state-sponsored user base, and explains why blocking malicious domains is no longer a useful defense.\\n\\nRead"}	verified	[]	ClickFix, initially a novelty in enterprise networks in late 2023, has transformed into a subscription-based product supported by blockchain infrastructure and user base, primarily targeting enterprises. The report explains that preventing attacks through domain blocking is no longer effective due to its sophisticated approach.	["ClickFix has become a prevalent method for attackers to enter enterprise networks", "It involves embedding malicious content into trustworthy websites", "It has evolved from a novelty to a subscription-based product with blockchain and user support", "Blocking malicious domains alone is no longer an effective defense against ClickFix"]	["ClickFix", "enterprise networks", "novelty in late 2023", "subscription-based product", "blockchain infrastructure", "ClickFix user base"]	["blockchain", "malware", "distributed systems"]	["cybersecurity", "enterprise networks", "malware", "ClickFix", "trust infrastructure"]	high	[]
https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/teamfiltration-compromises-seven.html	TeamFiltration Campaign Compromises Seven Microsoft 365 Accounts Using Default Passwords	https://thehackernews.com/2026/09/teamfiltration-compromises-seven.html	The Hacker News	2026-09-24 06:32:03+00	2026-09-30 16:05:31.357+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)", "snippet": "Cybersecurity researchers have disclosed details of an active TeamFiltration campaign codenamed UNK_CondorFiltration that has targeted over 5,700 accounts across 28 Microsoft 365 tenants.\\n\\nAccording to Proofpoint, the activity has primarily focused on Chilean retail and financial institutions. It originated from 1,487 unique AWS EC2 source IP addresses.\\n\\n\\"The campaign compromised 7 accounts –"}	verified	[]	A cybersecurity campaign named UNK_CondorFiltration has compromised seven Microsoft 365 accounts, targeting Chilean retail and financial institutions through over 5,700 accounts across 28 Microsoft 365 tenants. The campaign is believed to have originated from 1,487 unique AWS EC2 source IP addresses, most associated with Chile. Proofpoint reported it has primarily affected Chilean entities.	["Campaign UNK_CondorFiltration targeted Microsoft 365 accounts in Chile", "Compromised seven accounts", "Targeted over 5,700 accounts across 28 Microsoft 365 tenants", "Favoring Chilean retail and financial institutions"]	["UNK_CondorFiltration", "Microsoft 365", "Chile", "Proofpoint"]	["AWS EC2", "Microsoft 365"]	["cybersecurity", "infrastructure compromise", "Microsoft 365", "retail", "financial institutions", "AWS", "Chile"]	high	[]
