/node/25543	Eufy Omni C20, Omni X10 Pro	https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/ics-advisories/icsa-26-267-02	CISA Cybersecurity Alerts	2026-09-24 12:00:00+00	2026-10-02 16:05:31.031+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "CISA", "snippet": "View CSAF\\nSummary\\nSuccessful exploitation of these vulnerabilities could allow an attacker to run system level commands or execute arbitrary code.\\nThe following versions of Eufy Omni C20, Omni X10 Pro are affected:\\nOmni C20 <1.6.4 (CVE-2026-93289, CVE-2026-93290, CVE-2026-93291)\\nOmni X10 Pro <1.6.4 (CVE-2026-93289)\\nCVSS\\nVendor\\nEquipment\\nVulnerabilities\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nv3 9.4\\nEufy\\nEufy Omni C20, Omni X10 Pro\\nImproper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection'), Use of Ha"}	verified	[]	Exploitation of the mentioned vulnerabilities could allow attackers to run system level commands or execute arbitrary code on affected Eufy Omni C20 and Omni X10 Pro devices.	["Vulnerabilities affect Eufy Omni C20 and Omni X10 Pro devices", "Exploitation could result in running system level commands or executing arbitrary code", "Affected versions are Omni C20 <1.6.4 and Omni X10 Pro <1.6.4"]	["Eufy", "Eufy Omni C20", "Eufy Omni X10 Pro"]	["OS Command Injection"]	["Vulnerability", "Equipment", "Cybersecurity"]	high	[]
/node/25525	Siemens Desigo CC family	https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/ics-advisories/icsa-26-265-05	CISA Cybersecurity Alerts	2026-09-22 12:00:00+00	2026-10-02 16:05:31.031+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "CISA", "snippet": "View CSAF\\nSummary\\nA Client Code Execution (CCE) vulnerability has been identified in Desigo CC, potentially allowing malicious actors to execute arbitrary code on client devices through specially crafted graphics documents. This vulnerability leverages user-defined graphics containing embedded scripts that are executed on client application instances. Successful exploitation could lead to compromise of the client operating system and potential lateral movement within the organization.\\nThe follow"}	verified	[]	A vulnerability known as Client Code Execution (CCE) in Siemens Desigo CC allows attackers to execute arbitrary code on client devices via specially crafted graphics documents. Successful exploitation could compromise the client operating system and facilitate lateral movement in the organization.	["Desigo CC software", "Client Code Execution (CCE) vulnerability", "Graphics documents can be used to exploit the vulnerability", "Exploitation could lead to OS compromise and lateral movement", "Potential damage: Compromised client devices and possible access to broader systems"]	["Siemens Desigo CC", "Client Code Execution (CCE)", "Graphics documents"]	["Graphics programming", "Operating Systems and security"]	["vulnerability", "client devices", "security"]	high	[]
https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/police-arrest-16-year-old-suspected-of.html	Police Arrest 16-Year-Old Suspected of Running KillSec, Seize Ransomware Leak Site and Servers	https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/police-arrest-16-year-old-suspected-of.html	The Hacker News	2026-10-01 16:55:57+00	2026-10-02 16:05:30.97+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)", "snippet": "Police in Spain have arrested a 16-year-old whom investigators suspect of running the KillSec ransomware group. KillSec is accused of stealing data from organizations and threatening to publish it on its leak site unless they paid.\\n\\nThe 16-year-old was one of 3 people arrested on September 30, when police also took control of that site.\\n\\nInvestigators identified him as KillSec's suspected"}	verified	[]	A 16-year-old suspect in the KillSec ransomware group was arrested in Spain, along with two others. They were arrested on September 30 along with seizure of the leak site, ransomware group's control, and servers related to KillSec. The suspected individual is believed to have run the KillSec group, a cybercriminal operation involving data theft and extortion via ransomware.	["16-year-old arrested for suspected involvement in KillSec ransomware group", "Investigators suspect the individual operated the KillSec group", "Severe measures taken including arrest and confiscation of the group's servers and websites", "A total of 3 individuals were part of the operation", "The operation allegedly involved data theft and extortion via ransomware"]	["KillSec ransomware group", "16-year-old suspect", "Spanish police", "Investigators"]	["Ransomware", "Cybercrime"]	["Cybersecurity", "Law enforcement", "Child exploitation in technology"]	medium	[]
https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/how-financial-services-companies-can.html	How Financial Services Companies Can Modernize Their Software Supply Chain	https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/how-financial-services-companies-can.html	The Hacker News	2026-10-01 11:45:00+00	2026-10-02 16:05:30.97+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)", "snippet": "Every security leader at a bank, insurer, or asset manager has had a version of this conversation: Security wants to eliminate a class of vulnerabilities. Engineering explains what it would take to upgrade the platform where they live. Somebody prices out the regression testing. Somebody else raises the change-freeze calendar. The finding gets an exception, a compensating control, and a date"}	verified	[]	The financial services industry discusses the challenges of modernizing their software supply chain. Security and engineering teams frequently negotiate solutions to eliminate vulnerabilities, with processes for platform upgrades, regression testing, change-freeze calendars, and compensating controls often involved.	["Version of conversations at banks, insurers, and asset managers", "Elimination of vulnerabilities is a goal", "Engagement between security and engineering teams is central", "Processes like platform upgrades, regression testing, change-freeze calendars, and compensating controls are part of the discussions"]	["financial services companies", "banks", "insurers", "asset managers", "security leaders", "software supply chain", "class of vulnerabilities", "platform upgrade", "regression testing", "change-freeze calendar", "compensating control"]	["software development", "regression testing", "change management", "security controls"]	["security", "engineering", "financial services", "supply chain", "vulnerability management"]	medium	[]
