https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/why-cisos-struggle-to-answer-boards.html	Why CISOs Struggle to Answer the Board's Three Hardest Questions, and How to Fix the Report	https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/why-cisos-struggle-to-answer-boards.html	The Hacker News	2026-10-02 11:30:00+00	2026-10-02 16:05:30.97+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)", "snippet": "The quarterly board meeting is two weeks out. The security team is pulling exports from the identity provider, the cloud posture tool, the vulnerability scanner, the SIEM and the EDR console. Someone is building a spreadsheet to reconcile them. Someone else is turning that spreadsheet into slides.\\n\\nThen a board member asks three questions:\\n\\n\\n  How secure is the organization, overall?\\n  What is"}	verified	[]	This news snippet describes a scenario where a security team is preparing for a quarterly board meeting by exporting data from various security tools and reconciling it in a spreadsheet. A board member then asks three questions about the organization's security status and specific security controls.	["Security team preparing for a board meeting", "Exporting data from multiple security tools", "Reconciling data into a spreadsheet", "Board member asking security questions", "Board member inquiring about security overall and specific controls"]	["CISO", "Quarterly Board Meeting", "Security Team", "Identity Provider", "Cloud Posture Tool", "Vulnerability Scanner", "SIEM (Security Information and Event Management)", "EDR (Endpoint Detection and Response)", "Board Member", "Security Questions"]	["Data Exporting", "Spreadsheet", "Security Information and Event Management (SIEM)", "Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR)", "Board meeting preparation"]	["Security", "Board Meetings", "CISO Responsibilities", "Security Controls", "Security Questions"]	medium	[]
https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/threatsday-ai-powered-zero-day-chain.html	ThreatsDay: AI-Powered Zero-Day Chain, 543K Live Secrets, Model Inspection RCE and 13 More Stories	https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/threatsday-ai-powered-zero-day-chain.html	The Hacker News	2026-10-01 16:45:38+00	2026-10-02 16:05:30.97+00	cybersecurity	{"author": "info@thehackernews.com (The Hacker News)", "snippet": "This week, the useful words are boring ones: inspect, cache, compile, store, trust. Each sounds harmless. Each can become an attack path when a system does a little more than people expect. A model check can run code. A cache can mix up requests. A public secret can stay useful for years.\\n\\nThat is the lesson running through the list. Attackers do not always need a brilliant new trick. They can"}	verified	[]	This article discusses security threats associated with seemingly innocuous terms like inspect, cache, and store. It emphasizes that attackers often use simple vulnerabilities instead of developing new, sophisticated methods. The risks are highlighted across various system functions and storage types, such as models and secrets, which can be misused by attackers.	["Threats often arise from common-seeming security issues", "Attackers frequently exploit known vulnerabilities rather than create new ones", "Common system functions, such as model checks and caches, can be misused by attackers", "Vulnerabilities can persist for years, leading to compromised systems"]	["AI-Powered Zero-Day Chain", "13 More Stories", "Inspect", "Cache", "Store", "Trust", "Model Check", "Public Secret"]	["Model Inspection", "Cache", "Secret Storage"]	["Security", "AI", "Vulnerability", "Intrusion", "Persistence"]	medium	[]
